outputs/poster/layout_mockup.pdf

# ===========================================================================
# Other
# ===========================================================================
.archive/
*.backup
*.bak

# IEc handoff zip is re-buildable from the unpacked folder.
outputs/poster/iec_graphics_handoff.zip

# ===========================================================================
# v1.4.5 BRICK pipeline outputs (large; data on /scratch, summaries in CSV)
# ===========================================================================
# Slim per-arm weighted CSVs are 81 MB (LHS) to 435 MB (ANOVA) each — too big
# for git. Keep on disk for downstream plot scripts; pull from Torch when
# needed via rsync.
outputs/brick_v145_slim/*.csv

# Full Wong-pipeline weighted CSVs (460 MB LHS + 956 MB ANOVA each) likewise
# stay on /scratch + local disk only. The small per-arm envelopes (~500 KB)
# are kept in git for figure reproducibility.
outputs/brick_v145_summaries/*_weighted.csv

# Full per-cell BRICK driver outputs (~485 MB each) — these are the raw
# per-cell, per-component trajectories used by the brick_vs_grinsted TSLS
# component-decomposition figure and any per-cell L-T classification.
# Stay on Torch + local disk only; not committed.
outputs/brick_v145/*.csv

# Quarantined pre-fix outputs are large; the per-directory README is tracked
# but the CSVs themselves stay on local disk for postmortem only.
outputs/quarantine/**/*.csv
outputs/quarantine/**/*.npz

# Smoke / scratch outputs from the flatcube driver
outputs/flatcube_smoke_*.csv

# Obs-driven BRICK runs (large; reproducible via julia/run_mimibrick_obs_driven.jl)
outputs/brick_obsdriven_*.csv
parameters_subsample_sneasybrick.csv

# Diagnostic cross-posterior / sample-stability test outputs (large CSVs)
outputs/brick_xtest_prepr93/*.csv
outputs/brick_anova_stab_*.csv

# ===========================================================================
# v5 / Group-Sobol workstream large outputs (regenerable; data on Zenodo +
# Torch /scratch). The small per-axis decomposition CSVs and all figures ARE
# tracked; only the multi-GB raw ensembles and multi-MB regenerable metadata
# are excluded.
# ===========================================================================
# Raw per-cell BRICK trajectories for the LHS-10k_s noise-isolated ensemble
# (~2.4 GB). Regenerable via the BRICK arm; summaries/figures are tracked.
outputs/brick_v145_lhs10ks/
# Large regenerable ANOVA/feature metadata (rebuild via
# build_anova_factorial_metadata.py / build_rff_features_rich.py with fixed
# seeds). Small design metadata (<1 MB) stays tracked for reproducibility.
outputs/anova324k_brick_metadata.csv
outputs/anova324k_fair_metadata.csv
outputs/anova108k_brick_metadata.csv
outputs/lhs10ks_brick_postaugment_metadata.csv
outputs/rff_features_rich.csv

# Zenodo upload staging dir (transient; files live in the deposit)
outputs/_zenodo_staging_v5/

# Regenerable poster mockup/inset PDFs (PNGs are tracked for review)
outputs/poster/layout_mockup_42in.pdf
outputs/poster/E_pulse_slr_response_inset.pdf

# Print master: the vector PDF (poster_print_46x42.pdf) IS tracked — it is the
# upload deliverable. The full-size 300-DPI PNG (13800×12600, ~13 MB) is
# regenerable from the script, so it is NOT tracked.
outputs/poster/poster_print_46x42.png

# Large MCMC chain files (100s of MB each; regenerable via run_mcmc_*.sh).
# Keep adapted_cov*.csv, postprocess*.txt, log_* (small) tracked.
outputs/mcmc/chain_*_n*.csv
outputs/mcmc/archive_*/

# Large regenerable BRICK output data (Tier 3; regenerable from FaIR cubes / Zenodo)
outputs/rennels/
outputs/quarantine/
outputs/brick_2x2_clean/*.csv
# Office lock/owner files
~$*

# Dangendorf 2024 full field reconstruction (704 MB) — fetch-on-demand from
# Zenodo 10621070; the derived global-mean CSV (dangendorf2024_gmsl_annual.csv) is
# committed, so the pipeline runs without it. Re-fetch via the URL in
# python/diag_dangendorf_vs_frederikse.py header if you need the fields.
data/observations/raw/dangendorf2024_KalmanSmootherHR_Fields.nc

# Quarantined MCMC chains are large (0.8-1.4 GB each); keep provenance READMEs, not the chains
outputs/quarantine/**/chain_ext_seed*.csv

# GlacierMIP3 big per-experiment netCDF: untracked by design (re-fetch recipe in data/observations/raw/README_modern_extensions.md)
data/observations/raw/gmip3/GMIP3_reg_glacier_model_data/*.nc

# Gridded surface-temperature products (0.5 GB combined), fetch-on-demand from the
# providers. Only the DERIVED annual global-mean CSVs are committed, so nothing in
# the pipeline needs these. Untracked AND unignored, they made `git add -A` stall
# for long enough to look like a hang — see notes/handoff_2026-08-23d.
data/observations/raw/HadCRUT.5.0.2.0.analysis.anomalies.ensemble_mean.nc
data/observations/raw/Land_and_Ocean_LatLong1.nc
data/observations/raw/gistemp1200_GHCNv4_ERSSTv5.nc
data/observations/raw/gistemp1200_GHCNv4_ERSSTv5.nc.gz

# JPL GRACE/GRACE-FO mascon (44 MB) — the ONLY raw netCDF of its class that was ever
# tracked, and the largest tracked file in the repo. Same rule as its neighbours above:
# only the DERIVED products are committed (grace_{antarctica,greenland}_mass.txt and the
# LWS rows of recalib_targets_ext.csv), so nothing in the pipeline needs the raw grid.
# Consumed ONLY by python/build_lws_grace_extension.py via MASCON_GLOB, to REBUILD those
# derivatives. Re-fetch recipe in data/observations/raw/README_modern_extensions.md.
data/observations/raw/GRCTellus.JPL.*.GLO.RL06.3M.MSCNv04CRI.nc

# PROTECT-Greenland (Goelzer 2025) physics ensemble — 74 MB of tarballs + 3136
# unpacked NetCDFs. Fetch with scripts/fetch_protect_greenland.sh; provenance and
# md5s in data/comparison/protect_greenland/README.md. The DERIVED summary
# (outputs/protect_greenland_gis_summary.csv) IS tracked — that is the artefact.
data/comparison/protect_greenland/*.tgz
data/comparison/protect_greenland/IGE/
data/comparison/protect_greenland/IMAU/
data/comparison/protect_greenland/NORCE/
data/comparison/protect_greenland/VUB/
data/comparison/protect_greenland/info_p11/
data/comparison/protect_greenland/README.txt

# CMIP6 ssp585 EXTENSION tas (2101-2300), ~400 MB, re-fetchable:
#   bash scripts/fetch_cmip6_ssp585ext.sh   (md5s pinned in that script)
# The DERIVED outputs/cmip6_ssp585ext_gsat.csv IS tracked.
data/cmip6_gsat_ext/*.nc

# Large fetched ice-sheet archives (2026-08-22). Keep the README and PROVENANCE.txt
# tracked; the multi-GB zips/netCDFs are re-downloadable from the DOIs recorded there.
# Without this a `git add -A` sweeps in ~4.9 GB.
data/gis_post2100/**/*.zip
data/gis_post2100/**/*.nc
data/gis_post2100/**/*.pdf

# Greve run-spec headers: re-extractable from the tracked-DOI zip (run_specs_headers.zip).
# The experiment->GCM mapping they document is transcribed into EXPS in
# python/diag_gis_greve_year3000.py, which is where it needs to be readable.
data/gis_post2100/greve_chambers_2022/run_specs_headers/

# 1.8 GB of raw CMIP6 tas NetCDF for the Coulon ssp585 extension; the derived
# annual tables in data/cmip6_coulon/ are what is tracked (fetch_cmip6_coulon_ext.sh).
data/cmip6_coulon_ext/

# Pulse-pair ensembles and run logs: REGENERABLE-in-principle bulk, but far over GitHub's
# 100 MB per-file cap (12 files were ~1.2 GB each), so they can never be pushed. The pairs
# are kept as Parquet+zstd float32 instead -- 4.8x smaller, max relative error 6.0e-08,
# verified per file at conversion. The existing chain_* rule below did not cover either class.
outputs/mcmc/wong_cond_pulse_pairs_*.csv
outputs/mcmc/wong_cond_weights_full*.csv
outputs/mcmc/log_*.txt

# Per-draw SLR ensembles: INTERMEDIATES, not results (Marcus 2026-09-01 -- commit the code,
# the key results and the figures, not the intermediates). 96 files, 815 MB as CSV; kept on
# disk as Parquet+zstd float32 alongside. Removing this class from the pending outputs commit
# takes it from 741 files / 15.82 GB to 542 files / 34.9 MB.
outputs/scope_slr_fairunc_draws_*.csv
outputs/scope_slr_fairunc_draws_*.parquet
outputs/mcmc/wong_cond_pulse_pairs_*.parquet
outputs/mcmc/wong_cond_weights_full*.parquet

# Per-chain `ais_gmst_amp` column cache (diag_amp_by_vintage.py). An INTERMEDIATE, not a
# result: 7.6 MB per chain, 122 MB for the L21/L22/L23/L23b set, and regenerable from the
# chains in ~45 s each. The table it feeds, outputs/diag_amp_by_vintage.csv, IS committed.
outputs/mcmc/ampcol_*.npy

# Frozen in-tree copies of a driver, made so a long run cannot be disturbed by an edit
# to the live file (never_edit_a_running_script). They must live under julia/ because
# the drivers include ladrillo_projection.jl by @__DIR__; a copy in /tmp cannot resolve it.
julia/_frozen_*.jl

# Paired pulse cubes from build_fair_pulse_vv_v160.py: 56 files, ~200 MB. Left UNTRACKED
# (Marcus 2026-09-03) although the scenario cubes beside them are tracked -- they rebuild
# in ~1 min each from the FaIR driver, and the driver plus its gate log is the record.
data/observations/fair_cube_*_pulse*_2030_raw.csv

# Per-draw pulse dumps: 14 files, ~39 MB, and they exist so any statistic can be recomputed
# WITHOUT a re-run -- but that re-run is 15.6 min, so the same rebuildable-bulk rule the pulse
# cubes got applies. The cells / paths / gates files ARE tracked: they are the results, the
# figures read them, and together they are ~1 MB.
outputs/pulse_ladrillo_draws_*.csv
outputs/pulse_ladrillo_*_SMOKE.csv

# Mutation-test outputs are DELIBERATELY WRONG tables. The verdict lives in the
# commit message and CHANGELOG; the file itself must never be mistaken for a result.
outputs/*_MUT*.csv
